WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.950 --> 00:00:04.610
Friendship in reverse and a protagonist named
"The Protagonist"?

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.036
We won't blame you if you left Tenet with
more questions than answers!

00:00:09.032 --> 00:00:13.640
The Algorithm is the Macguffin of Tenet — which
in filmic terms, means - the thing everyone

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:14.522
is after.

00:00:14.522 --> 00:00:19.712
Many people die trying to locate it, and we're
told it holds the key to earth's destruction.

00:00:19.712 --> 00:00:22.090
But what exactly is the Algorithm?

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:27.060
One, it's a formula created by a scientist
in the future that allows time inversion,

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:32.041
and two, it is a physical doomsday device,
currently disassembled into multiple parts,

00:00:32.041 --> 00:00:35.071
which when assembled has enough power to destroy
the planet.

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:39.740
As explained by a scientist named Priya, who
believed the Algorithm was so dangerous that

00:00:39.740 --> 00:00:44.202
she created a physical copy that was then
dismantled and hidden all over the world — in

00:00:44.202 --> 00:00:45.202
the past.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:47.684
"There are nine nuclear bombs.

00:00:47.822 --> 00:00:49.213
Nine bombs.

00:00:49.292 --> 00:00:52.542
Nine sets of the most closely guarded materials
in the history of the world."

00:00:52.621 --> 00:00:55.760
In Tenet, we see the race to locate these
dismantled pieces.

00:00:55.859 --> 00:01:00.559
The clear villain is Sator who wants to reassemble
the device in order to destroy the world per

00:01:00.559 --> 00:01:02.810
instructions by a future source.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:07.150
The main hero of the film, typically known
as the protagonist in any story, is actually

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:11.082
referred to, within this film, by that very
title: the protagonist.

00:01:11.082 --> 00:01:12.014
"You've done your part."

00:01:13.022 --> 00:01:13.706
"My part?

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:16.759
I'm the protagonist of this operation."

00:01:16.759 --> 00:01:20.360
He wants to retrieve the Algorithm to prevent
the use of the device for the destruction

00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:24.707
of the world because he trusts that other
and better outcomes still remain possible.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.380
The Protagonist meets the character Priya
at four points in the story during which she

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:33.159
relays important information to him one piece
at a time.

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:37.960
Ultimately, Priya's plan to stop Sator involves
allowing the madman to locate and assemble

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.700
all the pieces to the Algorithm, but then
to stop him from using it, which is a bold

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:43.700
move, to say the least.

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:48.610
Luckily, the gamble pays off, Sator is defeated,
and everyone moves on with their lives.

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:53.110
However, in the film's closing moments, during
a final meeting between the Protagonist and

00:01:53.110 --> 00:01:54.732
Priya, he kills her.

00:01:54.732 --> 00:01:55.732
But why?

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:00.450
Well, it turns out that Priya had made plans
to try and kill Kat evidently believing such

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:04.530
action would preserve and protect the highly
confidential actions which had been taken

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:05.530
by Tenet.

00:02:05.530 --> 00:02:10.289
However, before she could pull off the stunt,
the Protagonist kills her to save Kat and

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:12.390
her son, who may actually be Neil.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:17.200
As it turns out, the Protagonist hired Priya
in the past and is actually the one responsible

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:18.043
for Tenet.

00:02:18.043 --> 00:02:22.010
Meaning that all along he wasn't working for
her, she was working for him.

00:02:22.654 --> 00:02:26.400
On a first viewing of Tenet, it's possible
to miss the part when Neil dies.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:29.569
To be fair, the final battle is difficult
to follow.

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:31.900
Repeated viewings make it clearer — or so
it seems.

00:02:31.900 --> 00:02:33.420
So, how does he die?

00:02:33.420 --> 00:02:38.930
Well, during that giant action finale at Stalsk-12,
the Protagonist makes his way to the hypocenter

00:02:38.930 --> 00:02:43.810
where Sator's man is busy lowering the Algorithm
into a massive hole next to a corpse with

00:02:43.810 --> 00:02:47.689
a familiar red tag lying on the other side
of a locked gate.

00:02:47.689 --> 00:02:51.810
Sator's man spots the Protagonist and is about
to shoot him when the corpse rises, takes

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.240
the bullet and opens the gate — in reverse.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.290
Turns out, this was Neil.

00:02:56.290 --> 00:02:59.890
Only, we didn't know that at the time because
the person was wearing a mask and the scene

00:02:59.890 --> 00:03:04.050
is intercut with seemingly simultaneous scenes
of Neil driving a jeep outside.

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:08.330
What we come to realize is that there are
multiple Neils performing multiple actions

00:03:08.330 --> 00:03:12.720
in multiple directions — some Neils are
inverted while others are moving forward.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.150
It turns out, Neil was also the man who saved
the Protagonist at the Kiev Opera.

00:03:17.150 --> 00:03:19.737
If that weren't enough, Neil reveals something
more.

00:03:19.737 --> 00:03:22.431
"You have a future in the past.

00:03:22.431 --> 00:03:23.444
Years ago for me.

00:03:23.444 --> 00:03:24.858
Years from now for you".

00:03:26.635 --> 00:03:27.879
"You've known me for years."

00:03:27.879 --> 00:03:32.030
They embarked on a number of grand adventures
before Neil finally met his end.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.030
So sad.

00:03:33.477 --> 00:03:36.340
So, what happened during the Kiev Opera siege?

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.930
After watching Tenet multiple times, we may
have the answer.

00:03:38.930 --> 00:03:43.230
Our hero, the Protagonist, arrives at a staged
terrorist attack.

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:47.280
Sator's crew are present in the form of disguised
SWAT team members and are there to retrieve

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:52.120
a piece of the Algorithm after which they
plan to destroy any evidence of its existence,

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.349
hence the bombs.

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:58.762
Real cops arrive to save the hostages, unaware
that Sator's men have infiltrated their SWAT unit.

00:03:58.762 --> 00:04:03.269
The Protagonist, who infiltrated Sator's fake
SWAT unit, thinks he's there to steal parts

00:04:03.269 --> 00:04:04.769
of a nuclear bomb.

00:04:04.769 --> 00:04:09.140
After meeting with a mysterious man in a box
seat, he learns the location of a package,

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:14.099
rushes downstairs and acquires what will ultimately
be revealed as a piece of the Algorithm.

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:16.250
But for now, he doesn't really know what it
is.

00:04:16.250 --> 00:04:20.799
Feeling heroic, the Protagonist returns inside
to help the hostages, runs into trouble, and

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:25.389
then gets saved by another masked individual,
only to ends up getting captured and tortured

00:04:25.389 --> 00:04:26.880
by Tenet for his troubles.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.035
Turns out, it was all part of a test.

00:04:29.035 --> 00:04:33.022
"That test you passed?

00:04:33.022 --> 00:04:34.299
Not everybody does."

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:38.789
At one point during Tenet, Kat reveals a moment
in the past where she witnessed a young woman

00:04:38.789 --> 00:04:41.043
jumping from her husband's yacht in Vietnam.

00:04:41.043 --> 00:04:45.410
Since the film deals with time travel, most
audience members correctly guess the mysterious

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.280
woman's identity as some sort of alternate
Kat.

00:04:48.280 --> 00:04:49.970
But how did she get there?

00:04:49.970 --> 00:04:53.970
Heavily referenced throughout the film, we're
told that Sator's happiest memory occurred

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:58.729
some time ago in Vietnam during which he and
Kat enjoyed a relaxing evening together on

00:04:58.729 --> 00:04:59.780
his yacht.

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:04.350
Future Sator ventures back to this time period
while past Sator is at the Kiev Opera to relive

00:05:04.350 --> 00:05:08.896
the memory before killing himself and setting
off events that will cause the end of the world.

00:05:08.896 --> 00:05:13.320
Luckily, the Protagonist, Neil, and Kat deduce
the time and place Sator will make his last

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:18.910
stand and send Kat backwards through time
to arrive at that same point, while also simultaneously

00:05:18.910 --> 00:05:20.882
healing her inverted bullet wound.

00:05:20.882 --> 00:05:25.800
Now it happens that future Sator is unknowingly
speaking with future Kat unaware that past

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:28.320
Kat has already taken their son to shore.

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:32.669
Past Kat then witnesses future Kat dive off
the yacht following Sator's execution at the

00:05:32.669 --> 00:05:34.289
hands of future Kat.

00:05:34.289 --> 00:05:39.169
Presumably, past Sator comes back from Kiev
and continues on with his life, unaware that

00:05:39.169 --> 00:05:41.427
his future self just died in the past.

00:05:42.045 --> 00:05:46.610
Here's a novel question for you: how did Sator
not know his plan had failed?

00:05:46.610 --> 00:05:51.250
He travels back to the past in order to execute
his plan, but since he still exists in the

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:52.577
future, shouldn't he know?

00:05:52.577 --> 00:05:56.610
"If you went back in time and killed your
own grandfather, how could you have been born

00:05:56.610 --> 00:05:57.610
to commit the act?"

00:05:57.610 --> 00:05:58.610
"What's the answer?"

00:05:58.610 --> 00:05:59.630
"There's no answer.

00:05:59.630 --> 00:06:00.639
It's a paradox."

00:06:00.639 --> 00:06:04.632
Tenet works around this concept by applying
a whole new set of time travel rules.

00:06:04.632 --> 00:06:09.949
As explained by Esquire, Christopher Nolan
utilizes the bootstrap paradox, or a causal

00:06:09.949 --> 00:06:12.950
loop in which an event causes another to occur.

00:06:12.950 --> 00:06:17.030
In Tenet, the Protagonist experiences the
events of the film, then relays the acquired

00:06:17.030 --> 00:06:21.915
information to Neil, who travels back in time
to affect those events with this acquired knowledge.

00:06:21.915 --> 00:06:26.266
Hence, the second event is causal to the first
event due to a loop continuum.

00:06:26.365 --> 00:06:27.415
Simple, right?

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:31.830
According to The Sign Post's Nic Muranaka
and Daryn Steed, the characters are stuck

00:06:31.830 --> 00:06:37.159
in a time loop, and having already gone back
to stop Sator they are forced to repeat stopping Sator.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:43.280
During the 747 heist and crash sequence at
Oslo Freeport, the Protagonist and Neil come

00:06:43.280 --> 00:06:47.040
across two doors with a red and blue marker
differentiating them.

00:06:47.040 --> 00:06:50.090
Inside, they find what appears to be the scene
of a crime.

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:54.280
There are bullet holes in the glass, gun parts
scattered on the floor and smoke hanging in

00:06:54.280 --> 00:06:55.000
the air.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.730
Neil asks his partner what happened, prompting
the Protagonist to reply that it hasn't happened yet.

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:02.772
Suddenly, a masked man leaps from both turnstiles.

00:07:02.772 --> 00:07:07.009
With all of the mysterious individual's actions
being inverted, the Protagonist is attacked

00:07:07.009 --> 00:07:09.000
while Neil is involved in a chase.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:13.870
Later, we discover that the inverted masked
man is actually a future version of the Protagonist.

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:18.419
He and Neil went back to this moment to use
the turnstile in order to un-invert themselves

00:07:18.419 --> 00:07:20.449
and return to the normal time flow.

00:07:20.449 --> 00:07:25.090
Our heroes inverted themselves in order to
save Kat from Sator's gunshot wound following

00:07:25.090 --> 00:07:26.760
the Tallinn freeway chase.

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:29.830
They also needed to go back to stop Sator
at Stalsk-12.

00:07:29.830 --> 00:07:34.009
However, in order to keep from disrupting
the past, and in service to the aforementioned

00:07:34.009 --> 00:07:38.669
bootstrap paradox, the Protagonist has to
use the turnstile without being seen by his

00:07:38.669 --> 00:07:39.669
past self.

00:07:39.669 --> 00:07:43.789
So, although he desperately tries to flee
his counterpart, he must engage in combat

00:07:43.789 --> 00:07:44.789
to escape.

00:07:45.315 --> 00:07:48.550
Neil and the Protagonist have a long and complicated
history.

00:07:48.550 --> 00:07:52.650
When they first meet up, Neil seems to know
a great deal about his new partner right down

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:55.430
to the type of soda he prefers — Diet Coke.

00:07:55.430 --> 00:08:00.479
He's also quick to trust the Protagonist despite
a relatively brief introductory period.

00:08:00.479 --> 00:08:04.560
Turns out, the pair have actually worked together
for some time, just not in normal terms.

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.720
At the end of the film, Neil explains that
this moment marks the beginning of their relationship.

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:13.069
Except, we know, in order to save his friend,
Neil is about to head off to certain death

00:08:13.069 --> 00:08:14.454
following this exchange.

00:08:14.454 --> 00:08:15.454
What gives?

00:08:15.553 --> 00:08:19.600
If one were looking at their relationship
from a chronological perspective, the Protagonist

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:24.730
we see at the end of the film presumably travels
back in time to begin Tenet and recruit Neil.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:28.620
Future-Protagonist and past-Neil then enjoy
a series of adventures before the former passes

00:08:28.620 --> 00:08:30.750
Neil off to his younger self.

00:08:30.750 --> 00:08:35.450
This is why Neil was able to save the Protagonist
at the Kiev Opera siege and during the Stalsk-12

00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:39.380
battle, because he already knew it was going
to happen based on information relayed to

00:08:39.380 --> 00:08:41.640
him by his partner from the future.

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:45.959
As explained by Insider, Neil is stuck in
a closed loop doomed to repeat the events

00:08:45.959 --> 00:08:49.742
of Tenet over and over, always leading to
his death.

00:08:49.742 --> 00:08:50.877
As Neil states:

00:08:51.390 --> 00:08:52.716
"What's happened happened."

00:08:53.774 --> 00:09:00.209
In the climactic battle at Stalsk-12, much
is happening — explosions, gunfire, helicopters,

00:09:00.209 --> 00:09:03.100
collapsing buildings — most of which is
inverted.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:07.350
Visually, Christopher Nolan doesn't do enough
to differentiate the good guys from the bad

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:10.650
which sometimes leaves us more confused than
elated.

00:09:10.650 --> 00:09:14.649
After repeated viewings, here's what we know
is happening during the grand finale: two

00:09:14.649 --> 00:09:18.930
private armies in league with Tenet run through
separate time periods in an attempt to create

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:23.980
a diversion so that the Protagonist, Neil,
and Ives can secure the Algorithm.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:27.870
The red team moves normally through time while
the blue team waits for the battle to result

00:09:27.870 --> 00:09:31.620
in the big explosion which is designed to
bury the Algorithm.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:34.670
The blue team then participates in the madness
in reverse.

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:39.520
Both teams unleash some serious firepower
and incur plenty of artillery themselves.

00:09:39.520 --> 00:09:42.650
We see people die, but we don't ever get a
good look at the enemy.

00:09:42.650 --> 00:09:46.839
The CIA handler at the beginning of the film
does admit he's not exactly sure who Sator

00:09:46.839 --> 00:09:48.490
is hiring to do his bidding.

00:09:48.490 --> 00:09:52.649
However, he does allude to the possibility
of them being Russian mercenaries, which makes

00:09:52.649 --> 00:09:57.170
sense considering Stalsk-12 is part of a collection
of closed Russian cities.

00:09:57.170 --> 00:10:00.700
In the end, all that matters is that Neil
and the Protagonist succeed.

00:10:00.700 --> 00:10:04.730
Which they do after supposedly slaughtering
hundreds of unseen bad guys.

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:08.015
So you can let your imagination fill in all
the bloody details.

00:10:08.693 --> 00:10:12.680
Tenet comes packed with a bunch of scientific
jargon that may be too hard to understand

00:10:12.680 --> 00:10:13.870
on the initial viewing.

00:10:13.870 --> 00:10:17.400
But, here's a hint that may help you: turn
on the subtitles.

00:10:17.400 --> 00:10:21.589
One such bit of mumbo jumbo involves something
called a temporal pincer, which is just a

00:10:21.589 --> 00:10:24.829
fancy way of describing a mission that utilizes
inversion.

00:10:24.829 --> 00:10:28.790
We get a good look at a temporal pincer in
the action-packed finale during which two

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:32.100
teams tackle the same battle from different
directions in time.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:36.040
The red team executes their mission by moving
forward, while the blue team waits for the

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:39.890
mission to end before inverting and fighting
the same battle backwards.

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:44.290
We see smaller temporal pincers occur throughout
the film, such as during the freeway chase

00:10:44.290 --> 00:10:46.180
and the 747 heist.

00:10:46.180 --> 00:10:50.680
But did you know the entirety of Tenet is
actually one large temporal pincer operation?

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:53.950
We learn as much during the closing scenes
where it's revealed the Protagonist is the

00:10:53.950 --> 00:10:59.890
one behind everything — and we mean everything,
Tenet, the operation, Neil, all of it.

00:10:59.890 --> 00:11:03.930
After experiencing the events of the film,
he goes back in time and sets up much of the

00:11:03.930 --> 00:11:06.529
film's actions in order to fulfill the time
loop.

00:11:06.529 --> 00:11:09.810
The Protagonist instructs people to execute
specific events.

00:11:09.810 --> 00:11:14.860
For example, Neil saving him at the Kiev Opera,
and later, allowing himself to be kidnapped,

00:11:14.860 --> 00:11:17.920
tortured, and recruited by the Tenet organization.

00:11:17.920 --> 00:11:22.751
He's not altering the past, per se, but executing
it the way it's supposed to be executed, as

00:11:22.751 --> 00:11:24.490
previously established.

00:11:24.490 --> 00:11:28.350
As in the film, perhaps the best way to understand
is to go back and watch it again with the

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:30.220
benefit of having been there before.

